<div class="prose">
<p>B’s first real build was an online community.</p>
<p>As a teenager, B was an obsessive newspaper reader and active STOMP forum contributor. When studying in Perth, she’d walk 20 minutes to Murdoch University every weekend for her Singapore news fix: the one physical copy of <em>The Sunday Times</em> flown in that morning on an SQ flight. This was before newspaper apps — B didn’t even have an iPhone yet, just an iPod touch she used to Skype home.</p>
<p>Spatchy started because B needed somewhere for her friends to continue hanging out after they — and eventually B herself — kept getting banned from STOMP, sometimes for what seemed like the dumbest reasons.</p>
<p>So she taught herself PHP, FTP, web hosting and vBulletin and built one.</p>
<p>Together with five volunteer moderators, all older than her, came an early education in running an online community: trolls, moderation, disputes and what the world would much later come to call Trust &amp; Safety.</p>
<p>She made older friends, learnt a lot about people, and discovered an instinct that would keep resurfacing: if the thing she needed didn’t exist, she could learn enough to build it.</p>
<p>Somehow, the little forum grew. At one point, Spatchy made it into Alexa’s top 100 websites in Singapore. It was approved for Google Ads and started making a little money. It also got B her first lawyer’s letter from SPH, and even earned a <a href="https://smong.net/2012/08/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mention</a> from <em>Phua Chu Kang</em> executive producer Ong Su Mann after he found his <em>New Paper</em> column being discussed there.</p>
<p>Not bad for something a teenager built because she and her friends kept getting banned from another forum. 😂</p>
<p>Spatchy didn’t ultimately survive the trolls, though pieces of it remain in the Wayback Machine. When B left university, she sold the site for a low four-figure sum.</p>
<p>It wasn’t a startup. It didn’t make B rich. But it was the first time she learnt that she could start with a problem, teach herself what she needed to know, build something from scratch — and have actual people use it.</p>
